  "narrative": "During the 1990s, Black and African women's hairstyles reflected a rich cultural heritage and a desire for self-expression. The afro, a hairstyle created by combing out natural growth of afro-textured hair, remained a staple in many communities. Cornrows, a traditional style of braiding practiced in Africa and the African diaspora, were also popular. In addition, box braids, which are considered a protective style, emerged as a trendy option for Black women. However, it's worth noting that documentation from this era is limited, but we can infer that these hairstyles played an important role in the cultural identity of Black and African women during the 1990s. The Jheri curl, a permanent wave hairstyle popularized in the 1980s, may have still been worn by some individuals during the early part of the decade, although its popularity was waning as more natural hairstyles gained traction. Overall, the hairstyles of Black and African women during this time period embodied a sense of pride, resilience, and community.",
